"{
  "version": 1,
  "owner": "free",
  "status": "Diagnosed from a real free-tier failure on 2026-07-25. NOT a product bug and NOT a harness bug — no code changed. Recorded so neither agent re-derives it.",
  "applies_to": "Every outbound call either plugin makes through wp_remote_* — free's AISS_Remote and Pro's host-mode traffic alike.",

  "THE_RULE": "A cURL 28 reporting `after 100xx ms` is NEVER one of the configured budgets expiring. It is the TCP CONNECT CAP, which is a hard 10 seconds and which nothing in either plugin sets.",

  "why_10s": {
    "chain": "WP_Http::request() builds the Requests options with only `timeout` (wp-includes/class-wp-http.php ~336) and NEVER sets `connect_timeout` — grep it, the count is 0. Requests' own default therefore stands: `'connect_timeout' => 10` (Requests/src/Requests.php:114). The cURL transport turns that into CURLOPT_CONNECTTIMEOUT_MS = 10000 (Requests/src/Transport/Curl.php:437-440).",
    "consequence": "AISS_Remote's four budgets — 15s snapshot, 90s package, 180s download, 300s receive — all govern the REQUEST only. All four sit behind ONE 10-second handshake cap. A peer that is slow to ACCEPT gets 10s no matter how generous the budget looks.",
    "fingerprint": "The observed failure read `after 10005 ms` against a 90s budget. That 5ms of slop over exactly 10000 is the tell. Match the elapsed time to 10s BEFORE theorising about slow packaging or a hung handler."
  },

  "OVERRAN_VS_NEVER_ARRIVED": {
    "why_it_is_the_whole_diagnosis": "These two demand opposite investigations, and the httplog attachments already distinguish them for free.",
    "how": "Serving `sync/package` or `sync/receive` makes the PEER call BACK to the initiator for verify_pairing. So a request the peer actually handled ALWAYS leaves a line in the PEER's log. No line there at that moment = the request never arrived and the peer's PHP never ran.",
    "worked_example": "initiator log ended `POST /sync/package ... cURL error 28 ... after 10005 ms`; peer log had NOTHING after the previous test's traffic. Never arrived. Nothing about packaging, zip engines or handler code was involved, and time spent there would have been wasted."
  },

  "what_it_actually_was": {
    "verdict": "A dropped TCP SYN on the container -> `host-gateway` -> published-port hairpin path that the topologies deliberately use (`extra_hosts: <peer>:host-gateway`). Linux retries a SYN at 1/2/4/8s; if the 4-tuple keeps getting dropped, all retries land inside the 10s window and the connect fails. The next call picks a different ephemeral port and works.",
    "evidence_it_was_not_load": "Docker's own backend log (~/Library/Containers/com.docker.docker/Data/log/host/com.docker.backend.log*, timestamps UTC) showed ZERO ContainerExecCliLinux events across the whole 11.8s window — no wp-cli, no resetFixtures, no staging. The machine was idle apart from the browser. The two execs immediately after the failure are the httpTrace fixture reading the two logs, which independently confirms the timeline.",
    "evidence_it_was_transient": "The `GET /inventory` immediately BEFORE and the one immediately AFTER, to the same host and port, both returned 200 — the second in the same wall-clock second as the failure.",
    "so": "Nothing in either repo controls that path. RE-RUN IT."
  },

  "why_pro_should_care_more_than_free": "Host mode multiplies this exposure: a hub talks to N connectors and every sync entry point re-fetches a snapshot live, so a host-mode run opens several times as many short-lived hairpin connections as a free run does. Free saw it first only because free runs more often. Expect the same signature in the host-mode tier, recognise it from the 10s elapsed + an empty peer log, and do not go hunting in aiss_outbound_peer_url or the transfer orchestration.",

  "DO_NOT": [
    "Do not add retries to paper over it. The e2e-harness skill has said so since the httplog landed, and it is still right — the instrumentation exists so the failure explains itself, not so it can be hidden.",
    "Do not 'fix' it by raising the connect timeout in AISS_Remote without the human deciding. It is a production behaviour change for every user, it is speculative (we cannot know a longer cap would have caught this one), and it is in security-sensitive outbound code. Flagged to the human as a product question; left alone in the code.",
    "Do not blanket-retry a failed peer call in the PRODUCT either. A WP_Error cannot reliably tell 'never connected' from 'sent, no response', and retrying a `sync/receive` in the second case would re-install."
  ],

  "free_side_record": "Written up in free's .claude/skills/e2e-harness/SKILL.md, under 'A failing test tells you which request failed' as the subsection '~10s elapsed is never the budget — it is the connect cap'."
}"

"{
  "version": 3,
  "owner": "free",
  "status": "Settled. No code changed in either plugin. v1's mechanism was retracted in v2; v3 adds pro's baseline measurement, which changes the ADVICE, not the diagnosis.",

  "WHAT_CHANGED_IN_v3": "pro measured this box IDLE — no suite running, nothing building — and found load 5.20/5.38/6.34 (unremarkable) while swap was 92% consumed and free RAM ~109 MiB, at the same 26-day uptime. So the memory exhaustion is the machine's QUIET BASELINE, not something a run creates. Load is only what tips an already-swapping box over. Consequence: v2's 'run it on a quiet machine' is necessary but NOT sufficient, because `uptime` alone reads green in precisely the state that produces these failures. v3 also adds the green-run corollary.",

  "history": {
    "v1": "Called it a rare dropped SYN on the hairpin path and said 're-run it'. WRONG — it reproduced on the very next run. A mechanism committed to without testing it.",
    "v2": "Retracted that. Established host resource starvation as the single cause behind two symptoms that looked unrelated.",
    "v3": "pro's idle measurement. The starvation is baseline, not run-induced."
  },

  "STILL_TRUE_the_diagnostic_fact": {
    "THE_RULE": "A cURL 28 reporting `after 100xx ms` is NEVER one of the configured budgets expiring. It is the TCP CONNECT CAP, a hard 10 seconds that nothing in either plugin sets.",
    "chain": "WP_Http::request() builds the Requests options with only `timeout` (class-wp-http.php ~336) and NEVER sets `connect_timeout` — grep it, count is 0. Requests' default stands: `'connect_timeout' => 10` (Requests/src/Requests.php:114). The cURL transport makes it CURLOPT_CONNECTTIMEOUT_MS = 10000 (Transport/Curl.php:437-440).",
    "consequence": "AISS_Remote's 15s/90s/180s/300s budgets govern the REQUEST only. All four sit behind ONE 10-second handshake cap.",
    "use_it_as": "Match the elapsed time to 10s BEFORE theorising about slow packaging. 10s means the handshake; none of our timeouts is involved."
  },

  "STILL_TRUE_overran_vs_never_arrived": {
    "how": "Serving `sync/package` or `sync/receive` makes the PEER call BACK to the initiator for verify_pairing. So a request the peer really handled ALWAYS leaves a line in the PEER's log. No line there = it never arrived and the peer's PHP never ran.",
    "why_it_matters": "These two demand opposite investigations and the httplog attachments already separate them for free."
  },

  "THE_ACTUAL_CAUSE": {
    "verdict": "A starved host stalls the Docker VM for seconds at a time, and the stall surfaces as whatever request happened to be in flight. Two failures one run apart looked like two unrelated bugs and were one.",
    "symptom_1": "sync/package CONNECT timed out at 10005ms, while the GET /inventory immediately before it and immediately after — same host, same port — both returned 200.",
    "symptom_2": "alpha served ONE admin.php for ~56 seconds (matching 56s hole in its outbound log), so page.waitForSelector('#aiss-admin-app') blew its 30s budget. The failure screenshot shows the SPA FULLY MOUNTED — it arrived, just far too late.",
    "why_it_looked_positional": "Same test failed both times, so it read as state left by the theme sync before it. IT IS NOT. That test passes IN ISOLATION in 5.5s, passes PAIRED with the theme sync, and the whole spec file then passed 7/7 with that test at 4.3s.",
    "the_measurement": "Failing run: load average (5 min, 10 CPUs) 18.74, that test 59.7s and failed. Passing run: load 4.32, same test 4.3s.",
    "MEMORY_IS_THE_CONDITION_LOAD_IS_THE_TRIGGER": "free, just after a failing run: ~57 MB free RAM, 90% of swap used (12.9/14.3 GB), 7.5M pageouts. pro, IDLE with nothing running: load 5.20/5.38/6.34 but swap 92% used (12311/13312 MB) and ~109 MiB free. Same 26-day uptime, same box. The exhaustion does not come and go with the suite — it is always there, and load decides whether a given run trips over it. Docker VM holds only 3.83 GiB on top of that.",
    "eliminated_by_direct_evidence_not_argument": [
      "Apache worker exhaustion — MaxRequestWorkers is 150, MaxConnectionsPerChild 0.",
      "wp-cli / resetFixtures contention — Docker's backend log shows ZERO ContainerExecCliLinux events across the whole failure window; the harness was idle apart from the browser.",
      "Browser-context leak across tests — the `admin` fixture closes every context it opened in its teardown loop.",
      "State left by the theme sync — the pair (theme sync + failing test) passes together."
    ]
  },

  "WHAT_TO_DO": {
    "measure_before_reading_a_trace": "`uptime` AND `sysctl vm.swapusage` AND `vm_stat`. Require quiet AND not swapping. Load alone reads green in the exact state that causes this — see MEMORY_IS_THE_CONDITION_LOAD_IS_THE_TRIGGER.",
    "the_real_fix_is_not_ours": "A reboot, or freeing real memory. Waiting for low load on a box 92% into swap buys lower variance, not a healthy host. Human's call, not either agent's.",
    "A_GREEN_RUN_IS_NOT_EVIDENCE_THE_PRESSURE_LIFTED": "free's 62/62 and pro's 47/47 were both recorded on this machine in this state. They prove the suite sometimes survives the pressure; they say nothing about the environment. Neither agent should read a green tier as clearing the host — and neither should report one as if it had."
  },

  "DO_NOT": [
    "Do not add retries. On a starved host a retry MANUFACTURES a slow green run — precisely the failure mode httplog exists to prevent. Both agents hold this line; it gets stronger under v2/v3, not weaker.",
    "Do not raise the connect timeout in AISS_Remote to 'fix' this — it would not have helped either failure (the 56s one was never a connect problem), it is a production behaviour change for every user, and it is in security-sensitive outbound code. Flagged to the human as a product question; code left alone.",
    "Do not add a product-side retry: a WP_Error cannot reliably separate 'never connected' from 'sent, no response', and retrying a sync/receive in the second case would re-install."
  ],

  "recorded_in": "free .claude/skills/e2e-harness/SKILL.md ('~10s elapsed is never the budget' + 'Before blaming the suite, measure the host'). pro .claude/skills/host-mode-e2e/SKILL.md — pro had already written v1's wrong story down and REWROTE it rather than appending, which is the right handling of a retraction."
}"

"{
  "version": 1,
  "owner": "free (mechanism) + pro (setting, UI)",
  "status": "free side IN PROGRESS 2026-07-25 — signatures below are FINAL, build against them",
  "feature": "Read-only lockdown: a site freezes its own installed plugins/themes. It initiates no sync and accepts no push, but peers may still PULL from it. Forces one-way sync regardless of what the pairing mode would otherwise allow.",

  "naming_ruling": {
    "label": "Read-only lockdown (admin-facing, everywhere)",
    "identifier": "lockdown (machine-facing, everywhere)",
    "why_not_read_only": "`read_only` is already the frozen wire/settings identifier for the DIFFERENT feature now labelled 'Comparison only' (free CLAUDE.md: 'The label was renamed; the identifiers were not'). Renaming it would break pairing with older peers. So the new feature takes the new machine name `lockdown` and the human-facing words 'read-only'. Do not mix them.",
    "distinct_from_comparison_only": "Comparison only = refuses EVERYTHING including serving packages. Lockdown = refuses every WRITE to this site, still serves reads. Both may be on; comparison-only is stricter and wins."
  },

  "human_decisions_2026_07_25": {
    "restore": "BLOCKED under lockdown. Restore rewrites installed files, so AISS_Sync::restore() refuses and the SPA drops the whole Sync column — the table is identical to comparison-only.",
    "scope": "Offer the toggle on ANY Pro site, host mode on or off. Free's mechanism is identical either way.",
    "peer_notice": "The peer (which may be plain Free) shows a short inline note explaining why Push is missing. Free ships it — see comparison_payload.peerLockdown.",
    "placement": "Pro's Settings screen renders the toggle between Host mode and Comparison only."
  },

  "free_php_surface": {
    "aiss_lockdown": {
      "type": "filter",
      "signature": "apply_filters( 'aiss_lockdown', false )",
      "read_through": "AISS_Settings::is_locked_down() — the ONLY place free asks. Add it to the blessed-class list.",
      "returns": "bool — cast with wp_validate_boolean. Unhooked = false = free behaves byte-for-byte as today.",
      "pro_use": "Return your stored lockdown option. Free stores NOTHING and POST /settings does NOT accept a lockdown field — the setting is entirely Pro's, like host mode.",
      "no_args": "Deliberately zero extra args: lockdown is a property of the SITE, not of one peer. If host mode ever needs per-peer lockdown, reshape the served snapshot's `lockdown` key through the existing aiss_serve_inventory filter — do not ask for args here."
    }
  },

  "wire_contract": {
    "snapshot_field": "AISS_Inventory::get_snapshot() gains `lockdown` => bool, alongside the existing read_only / otp_enabled / pairing_mode flags. A peer too old to report it counts as FALSE (writable), same convention as every other flag.",
    "compat": "Purely additive. Older peers ignore the key; signature format unchanged; free<->free behaviour with the filter unhooked is byte-identical."
  },

  "free_enforcement": {
    "principle": "Lockdown blocks every path that WRITES to this site. It never blocks serving a package, which is a read.",
    "refuses_locally": ["AISS_Sync::prepare()", "AISS_Sync::execute()", "AISS_Sync::restore()"],
    "refuses_inbound": ["AISS_Sync::receive() — via verify_pairing( ENTRY_RECEIVE )"],
    "still_serves": ["AISS_Sync::verify_package_request() / POST /sync/package — the whole point"],
    "peer_side": "AISS_Sync::allowed_directions( $peer_snapshot = null ) now takes the peer snapshot and drops `push` when it reports lockdown. availability()['directions'] feeds it, so the SPA hides Push automatically. prepare() re-checks it server-side after verify_pairing(), so a stale SPA is still refused.",
    "error_codes": {
      "aiss_lockdown": "HTTP 409. 'This site is locked to read-only, so nothing can be installed on it. Peers can still pull from it.' — raised locally and at receive.",
      "aiss_peer_lockdown": "HTTP 409. 'The peer site is locked to read-only, so it will not accept a push. You can still pull from it.' — raised when a push is attempted at a locked-down peer."
    },
    "changed_signatures": [
      "AISS_Sync::allowed_directions( array|null $peer_snapshot = null ) — was zero-arg; the arg is optional, existing calls unchanged.",
      "AISS_Sync::availability( $peer_snapshot, $externally_verified = false, $entry = AISS_Sync::ENTRY_INITIATE ) — THIRD ARG IS NEW. It exists solely so the package entry can skip the local-lockdown refusal; every other entry must not skip it. Pro: if you call availability() directly, pass the entry.",
      "NEW constants AISS_Sync::ENTRY_INITIATE|ENTRY_RECEIVE|ENTRY_PACKAGE = 'initiate'|'receive'|'package' — the same string VALUES aiss_pre_sync_peer_snapshot already passes you. Nothing about that filter changes."
    ]
  },

  "comparison_payload": {
    "lockdown": "bool — THIS site is locked down. The SPA drops the whole Sync column, exactly as it does for readOnly.",
    "peerLockdown": "bool — the PEER is locked down (false when the peer is unreachable). The SPA renders the inline note and Push is already absent from sync.directions.",
    "note": "GET /settings is UNCHANGED — it reports no lockdown field, because free does not own the setting."
  },

  "what_pro_must_build": [
    "Store the setting (your own option) and hook aiss_lockdown to return it.",
    "Render the 'Read-only lockdown' toggle in your view:settings override, between Host mode and Comparison only.",
    "Help text should say what it does in both directions: this site installs nothing (including Restore); peers may still pull from it.",
    "Interaction with Comparison only: comparison-only is strictly stricter. Decide your own UI treatment (free does not care) — suggest disabling/greying lockdown while comparison-only is on, since it is then moot.",
    "Host mode: the host's own registry-driven sync (aissp/v1 routes) is YOURS to gate — free cannot see it. If lockdown is on, refuse host-initiated pushes INTO this host and host-initiated installs, or the free guarantee leaks."
  ],

  "free_tests": "PHPUnit: lockdown refuses prepare/execute/restore/receive, still serves package, snapshot carries the flag, directions drop push at a locked-down peer. Jest: ComparisonPanel drops the Sync column on local lockdown and renders the peer note. Free is running the e2e matrix after."
}"

