    {
      "claim": "'Backups' presented as a backup product",
      "truth": "They are pre-sync copies of the one plugin or theme being replaced, capped at five, pruned on write. Not a site backup, not a database backup, no scheduling, nothing offsite.",
      "two_further_traps": [
        "A sync that INSTALLS something the receiving site does not have backs up nothing — there is nothing to save (AISS_Installer::create_backup() returns null on a fresh install). So 'backup before every sync' is false; use 'before anything is overwritten'. The same defeats 'restore any item': only items a sync overwrote have a backup, and free-03 in the shot set visibly shows one row with Restore and the rest without.",
        "Deactivating Pro collapses an item's history from five to one THE NEXT TIME THAT ITEM IS SYNCED — not at deactivation. Phrase it that way; the naive version is friendlier and wrong."
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Read-only lockdown presented as security hardening",
      "truth": "Not a firewall, not malware protection. It freezes what Syncinator may change, and approved peers can still PULL from a locked site, because serving a copy is a read."
    },
    {
      "claim": "'Freeze a site so nothing can be installed on it' — lockdown as a WordPress-wide install block",
      "truth": "FALSE, and pro rates this the most dangerous item on either list. Every enforcement point is inside AISS_Sync; free hooks none of core's install paths — no map_meta_cap, no user_has_cap, no upgrader_pre_install, no file_mod_allowed. An administrator on a locked-down site can still use Plugins → Add New and install anything. Any phrasing of the form 'nothing can be installed on it' must be scoped to Syncinator. It also drops half the switch: a locked site starts no syncs of its own either.",
      "why_it_nearly_shipped": "Writing for a non-technical audience is exactly what produced it. The plainer the language, the wider the promise."
    },
    {
      "claim": "'Pro adds multisite support'",
      "truth": "Free supports multisite on its own. Pro adds PER-SUBSITE PAIRINGS — free implements the whole mechanism, Pro owns the switch."
    },
    {
      "claim": "'Each subsite gets its own Syncinator' read as file isolation — free's amendment",
      "truth": "A subsite does NOT get its own copies of plugins and themes. WordPress has ONE wp-content/plugins for an entire network; per-blog activation is real, per-blog files are not, and no plugin can change that. 'Its own Syncinator' means its own pairing — peer, key, settings, log, backups — never its own files.",
      "why": "A buyer who reads isolation into it discovers otherwise on their first conflict, which is a refund. It is published as an FAQ answer rather than only a negative. Related: the ownership ledger is a Syncinator-only lock and must never be described as protection — Network Admin can still update or delete a locked item, and a WordPress auto-update rewrites it regardless."
    },
    {
      "claim": "'Staging → production migration', whole-site moves, arbitrary version rollback, one-click cloning",
      "truth": "It does not move a site. Restore chooses among stored pre-sync backups of one item."
    },
    {
      "claim": "'Update every site in one pass' / 'bring everything into line' — Sync All's scope",
      "truth": "prepare_batch( $peer_url, $type, $slugs, $direction ) — inc/class-aissp-host-sync.php:122. Scoped to ONE connected site, ONE type, ONE direction. False three ways at once: plugins and themes are separate runs, push and pull are separate runs, and it is one site not all of them. Completeness promises also overclaim — locked or refused items are withdrawn from the plan, and an item that cannot be prepared is reported and stepped over rather than failing the run."
    },
    {
      "claim": "'Every site that asks to join waits for your approval'",
      "truth": "False whenever the connect passcode is on: a site proving it knows the rotating passcode is approved without the admin acting on that connection. Accurate shape: a connection either waits for your approval, or proves it knows your passcode. Both halves, or it overclaims."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Cache lifetime as a Pro feature",
      "truth": "Pro does not extend aiss_cache_ttl_choices at all. Free's two choices — off and 1 hour — are what Pro sites get. Pro's extension bundle carries a CACHE_TTL_CHOICES constant that MIRRORS free's so the host Settings card can draw the dropdown; it is not an extension. Its absence from any Pro list is the accurate state, not a simplification. (Log retention IS extended; the two dropdowns look identical and only one is a delta.)"
    },
    {
      "claim": "'One click' anywhere near a sync",
      "truth": "A single-item sync is click-then-confirm; Sync All shows a plan and waits for a go-ahead; with passcodes on it is two people. 'One action', never 'one click'. This defect reached three places at once — the page H1, a bullet, and free's own upsell button, which read 'One-click sync with PRO' and was corrected to 'Sync all at once with PRO' (app/src/components/BulkSyncUpsell/index.jsx) after pro ruled it a false Pro claim."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Security promises of the form 'nobody can push code to your site'",
      "truth": "This is not a security product. The honest ceiling: a sync requires the peer to be approved in the registry or verified by passcode, every request is signed, and the host re-verifies against its registry with a live back-fetch before acting. What is verified is possession of the shared key — NOT where a request came from; nothing checks origin. And signing alone never grants a write: installing anything additionally requires both sites to name each other. The line that survives all of it: 'A stolen key can read. It can't install anything.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "'Comparing writes nothing to either site'",
      "truth": "Narrow it to 'cannot change what is INSTALLED on either site'. Two real exceptions, both storing data about the comparison rather than code: a one-way private site pushes its own inventory to the source on every refresh, and a Pro host caches each peer's snapshot and stamps last_seen on contact. Verified independently on both sides; one phrasing covers both halves."
    }
  ],

  "layout_constraint_not_copy": {
    "fact": "Pro registers NO admin menu of its own. Pro surfaces INSIDE free's existing Syncinator screen. The standalone SPA under Pro's app/src/ is dormant scaffolding nothing enqueues.",
