"{
  "summary": "The mail-path enforcement filter for Advanced routing — phase 2, confirmed with the human operator. Supersedes the 'no filter exists yet' note in php.advanced-routing-settings for the enforcement question specifically (get_settings() itself is unchanged, still a plain static call).",
  "filter": "aieh_advanced_routing_decision",
  "signature": "apply_filters( 'aieh_advanced_routing_decision', array( 'action' => 'hold', 'forward_to' => '' ), $recipient_email )",
  "fired_from": "AIEH_Mail_Interceptor, at each of its 4 existing call sites (on_wp_mail, on_pre_wp_mail, enforce_hold, on_phpmailer_init) — computed as a pure function of the message + current settings, independently at each site, the same way is_blocking() already is today. Two call sites can see this filter fire for the same message under different SMTP-plugin hook orders (see .claude/skills/mail-interception/SKILL.md); your callback must be a pure function of $recipient_email and your own stored settings, with no side effects and no per-call state, or the two evaluations can disagree.",
  "preconditions_free_guarantees": [
    "Only called while the global block_email switch is ON. If it's off, this filter never fires at all — Advanced routing cannot act regardless of what your callback would return.",
    "Only called when the message has exactly ONE recipient. A message with 2+ recipients in `to` never reaches this filter — Advanced routing has no effect on it for v1, confirmed scope with the operator. Do not attempt to handle multi-recipient in your callback; you will never see one here."
  ],
  "return_shape": {
    "action": "'hold' | 'allow' | 'forward' — anything else is treated as 'hold'",
    "forward_to": "only read when action is 'forward'. An empty string or unparsable address is treated as 'hold', not as 'allow' — a broken forward config must not silently deliver to the original recipient, since the administrator's forward rule means they explicitly did not want that."
  },
  "effect_of_each_action": {
    "hold": "identical to today's plain global-block behavior for this message",
    "allow": "message delivers to $recipient_email unchanged, despite the global switch being on",
    "forward": "message delivers, but `to` is rewritten to forward_to before free's log captures it — the log shows the address it actually went to, not the original. Logged as status 'sent', not a new status; forwarding is a delivery variant, not a 4th outcome, matching the existing sent/blocked/failed model."
  },
  "your_side": "Register with add_filter('aieh_advanced_routing_decision', callback, 10, 2), unconditionally (not gated behind any other precondition) — mirrors how Syncinator's aiss_lockdown is registered unconditionally in AISSP_Loader. Your callback reads AIEHP_Advanced_Routing_Settings::get_settings(), resolves $recipient_email to a WP user via get_user_by('email', ...) if one exists, and checks that user's ID / any of their roles against the active mode's list. An email with no matching WP account can never match a user-type or role-type list entry — it simply falls through to 'not matched' for whichever mode is active (held in allow/forward mode, delivered in block mode) — this is a natural consequence of recipient-based matching, not something free needs to special-case.",
  "status": "Design confirmed with operator 2026-08-05, not yet implemented on either side. Free is implementing the interceptor side now, with full test coverage before merge — this file (AIEH_Mail_Interceptor) does not ship changes without that."
}"

